// Redundant read after read elimination, under the Java Memory Model.
//
// Source: Ševčík, "Program Transformations in Weak Memory Models" (PhD thesis,
//         Edinburgh 2008), §5.3.2. TC18 of Pugh's causality test cases is
//         similar (Jeffrey & Riely, "On Thin Air Reads", Example 3.7).
//
// The JMM FORBIDS r2 = 1 in this program: the only committing sequence that
// reaches r2 = 1 commits the race on x with value 1, then the race on y with
// value 1, and after the restart r3 := y can only see the default 0, so x := r3
// writes 0 and breaks the commitment to write 1. Rewriting r3 := y to r3 := r2
// keeps that commitment, so the transformed program allows r2 = 1, and the JMM
// invalidates redundant read after read elimination.
//
// litmus-tests/sevcik_thesis/Redundant Read after Read Elimination.lit is the
// same program asserting allow, which is sMRD's verdict.
//
x := 0;
y := 0;
{
  r1 := x;
  y := r1
} ||| {
  r2 := y;
  if (r2 = 1) {
    r3 := y;
    x := r3;
  } else {
    x := 1
  }
}
%%
forbid (r2 = 1) [JMM] "This result is not possible in this program, but it becomes possible after rewriting r3=y to r3=r2."
